{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Root-cause investigation — cause of death CAPTURED and reproduced\n\nInvestigated on a local py3.11.13 venv with the repo's CI flags.\n\n### 1. The worker's cause of death: pytest-timeout's `thread` method calls `os._exit(1)`\n\nCI runs the suite with `--timeout=300 --timeout-method=thread` (`.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml`\nlines 696/711). pytest-timeout's `thread` method, on expiry, dumps stacks to the worker's **captured**\nstderr and then calls `os._exit(1)` — which does not flush, does not unwind, and does not raise. Under\nxdist the execnet channel dies mid-test and the captured dump is discarded.\n\nI reproduced the EXACT ticket signature with a throwaway `time.sleep(60)` test under\n`--timeout=5 --timeout-method=thread -n 2`:\n\n    [gw1] node down: Not properly terminated\n    worker 'gw1' crashed while running 'test_hang.py::test_hangs'\n\nNote what is ABSENT from that output and equally absent from the CI logs: no traceback, no\n`Timeout >300.0s` line, no faulthandler dump, no `Fatal Python error`, no `INTERNALERROR`. The\nticket's three \"missing marker\" observations are all explained by this one mechanism.\n\n**AC #2 classification: DELIBERATE PROCESS EXIT** (pytest-timeout watchdog `os._exit`), not an OOM\nkill, not a native abort, not an xdist protocol failure.\n\nThis also matches the timing evidence already on the ticket: the crashing jobs ran 879s and 902s\nagainst ~610-640s for sibling legs — a delta of ~240-290s, i.e. one test burning very close to the\nfull 300s budget before the watchdog fired.\n\n### 2. A proven unbounded-wait defect on the exact code path this test uniquely exercises\n\n`test_matching_guard_is_accepted` is the ONLY test in `tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py` that\nreturns while a job is still IN FLIGHT. Every other test either polls to a terminal status\n(`_poll`) or enqueues nothing (403/400/404 cases). So it is the only test whose `client` fixture\nteardown runs concurrently with a live job.\n\n`src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py` `lifespan()` shuts down like this:\n\n    for task in tasks:\n        task.cancel()\n    for task in tasks:\n        with contextlib.suppress(asyncio.CancelledError):\n            await task          # UNBOUNDED\n\n`_worker` runs the job as `await asyncio.to_thread(jobs.run_job, ...)`. Cancelling the task does not\nstop the thread — `to_thread` work is not cancellable — and the event loop's teardown then joins the\norphaned thread via `loop.shutdown_default_executor()`. Measured directly with a probe that enqueues\na job and immediately exits the `TestClient` context:\n\n- job sleeps 30s, `job_timeout_seconds=30` -> teardown returned at **30.02s**\n- job sleeps 12s, `job_timeout_seconds=30` -> teardown returned at **12.01s**\n\nTeardown tracks the JOB duration, not the configured job timeout, which pins the wait on the\nexecutor join rather than on `asyncio.timeout`. There is no upper bound: an in-flight job wedges\nlifespan shutdown for as long as it runs. Reproduced identically on 3.11.13 and 3.12.13.\n\nThis is a known trap in this codebase and the sibling app already guards it.\n`src/rebar/review_bot/app.py` bounds precisely this wait, with a comment saying an\n\"unbounded `await task` hang shutdown with no upper bound\", and awaits the join under\n`shutdown_cancel_seconds()`, ABANDONING whatever is still pending. `tests/conftest.py` even carries\nan autouse `_bound_review_bot_shutdown_drain` fixture so tests do not inherit that app's production\ndrain. `opcert_service.app`'s docstring says it mirrors `review_bot.app`'s pattern — it copied the\nACK-fast queue+worker shape but NOT the bounded shutdown.\n\n### 3. What is NOT yet proven — stated plainly\n\nIn this test `jobs.run_job` is monkeypatched to `_fake_completed`, which is pure and instant\n(`new_record` is a dict literal). So I have NOT reproduced a 300s stall in the test as written, and I\nhave NOT established what makes the in-flight job slow on a loaded CI runner. Naive repeats do not\nreproduce it: the module looped 12x under `-n 4 --dist worksteal` and a generated 400-copy stress\nfile both passed clean.\n\nSo the honest state is: the KILL mechanism is proven, and an unbounded wait on the exact code path\nthis test uniquely exercises is proven, but the link between them (what makes the job slow enough to\nreach 300s) is inferred, not demonstrated.\n\n### Bearing on the fix\n\nThe remediation follows from what IS proven rather than from the unproven link: give the op-cert\nservice the same bounded shutdown its sibling already has, so an in-flight job can never wedge\nlifespan shutdown without an upper bound. That is a real production defect in its own right — a\ndeploy-time SIGTERM today blocks for the full duration of a running gate job (a git clone + SSM\nfetch + LLM call), not for a bounded grace window — and it removes the only unbounded wait reachable\nfrom this test, whatever makes a job slow. It is a mechanism fix, not a retry, skip or quarantine.\n\nNo code changed yet."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786518144375713001,"uuid":"4d5e977f-9661-4d7e-8d51-f6d00c7c39d1"}